fix(domain): on Effect Cluster, fence a commit on its runner's shard lock

Against alchemy `packages/domain/src` at 33c10dd (2.0.0-beta.87) with
`03-commit-version-check.patch` applied first: it extends 03's refusal of a
commit. It needs `08-name-the-runner-pool.patch` (in `packages/effect-cluster`)
for `runnerPoolName`. Apply from the repository root with `git apply`; the text
above the first `---` line is ignored. No hunk overlaps 01, 02 or 06.

## Problem

With 03, two runners holding one instance while its shard moves no longer lose
an acknowledged write: their commits serialize on the instance's advisory lock
and the later one fails its version check. But they are ordered by
transaction, not by ownership. A runner that stalls inside its commit (a GC
pause, a partition, a frozen container) past its shard lock, while another
runner takes the shard and writes, is still acknowledged when it resumes,
because its transaction began first.

How Effect Cluster 4.1.4 records ownership on PostgreSQL
(`packages/effect/src/cluster/SqlRunnerStorage.ts`, `Sharding.ts`):

- each shard is a session-level advisory lock,
  `pg_try_advisory_lock(namespace, (groupIndex + 1) * 1000000 + shard)`,
  with the namespace an FNV-1a hash of `effect-cluster:<prefix>`, held on one
  reserved connection per runner (`acquireLockConn`, `pgLocks`);
- that session's `idle_session_timeout` is the lock expiry (35 s by default),
  so a frozen runner's session ends and its locks are released;
- a runner is listed only while its heartbeat in `cluster_runners` is within
  the same expiry (`getRunners`), and shards are assigned over the listed
  runners (`Sharding.ts`, the hash ring); so 35 s into a freeze another
  runner is assigned the shards and takes their locks.

A domain commit runs on another connection (the domain's own pool). It cannot
take or share the shard's lock, whose holder is another session; it can read
it in `pg_locks`.

## Change

`objectStorage.transaction` fences every transaction, after the instance's
advisory lock (03):

- first, it finds the session named after this runner that holds the
  instance's shard lock (see below), and records that session's pid in a
  transaction-local setting, `alchemy.commit_fence`; with none, the
  transaction dies with `ShardLost` before it writes;
- a deferred constraint trigger on the storage table (`AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE`,
  `WHEN (NEW.key = 'meta')`, `INITIALLY DEFERRED`) checks at `COMMIT` that the
  same pid still holds the lock, and otherwise raises SQLSTATE `AD001`, which
  aborts the commit; the storage reports it as `ShardLost`. The migration
  creates the trigger and its function, one runner at a time.
- `commit` turns `ShardLost` into the call's failure, as it does 03's
  `CommitConflict` (`reportDefectsUnlessRefused`): never acknowledged, retried
  by the caller under the same command id, starting from the receipt lookup.
- Each refusal is logged (`commit fenced: ...`).

The check at `COMMIT` runs after the last point where the runner itself can
stall. A takeover can complete only inside the server's own commit, between
the trigger and the commit record; even then the new owner cannot commit to
the instance before this commit is visible (03's advisory lock), and its own
commit then fails 03's version check.

## Why it is safe, and what it needs

- Durable Objects (Cloudflare) and Rivet are unchanged: the fence is in the
  Effect Cluster storage only, and their storage already admits one instance
  at a time. The in-memory platform is unchanged.
- A commit that is refused writes nothing, and its retry reaches the current
  owner: the same path as 03's `CommitConflict`.
- Which session is this runner's: the one named after it. 08 names the pool
  Effect reserves the lock session from `effect-cluster:<host>:<port>`, from
  the address the runner gives Effect, and the fence computes the same name
  from `ShardingConfig.runnerAddress` (`runnerPoolName`). There is no
  fallback to the session's address or to any holder: behind a proxy or load
  balancer every session has the same address, and taking another runner's
  session for this one's is how a stale runner was acknowledged.
- A runner whose pool is not named (a host that builds its own pool rather
  than through the bootstrap) has no session of its own, so every commit it
  makes is refused and retried until the caller gives up. That is the safe
  failure, and the one to send: it never acknowledges a write it cannot
  attribute. It is also silent apart from the `commit fenced` lines, so a
  host built that way should fail loudly at startup instead; the bootstrap is
  the only path Alchemy's Effect Cluster platform takes, so none does today.

## Tests

- In memory (two in-process runners on one instance's storage, the host
  fencing a transaction whose runner no longer serves the instance when it
  commits): runner 0 decides, the instance moves to runner 1, runner 0's commit
  returns with the version unchanged. Without the fence it is acknowledged;
  with it, it is refused and its retry commits on runner 1.
- On Effect Cluster with Postgres, with 08 (two runners on a local swarm, a
  runner frozen with `docker pause` inside its commit until another runner
  holds its shard; a write sent to the frozen runner at once, and another
  after the shard moved): in six runs the frozen runner's commit was refused
  at `COMMIT` every time, and every write the resumed runner decided before it
  took its shard back was refused at the fence's first read ("does not hold
  shard"). Each refused write was committed once, by whichever runner's own
  session held the shard at the time, the last 12.03 s after the unpause (the
  derived bound is 33 s). The phase 10 version of this fence, which fell back
  to the session's address, acknowledged such a write in three of five runs.
- After the lock session is ended (`pg_terminate_backend`), the session Effect
  rebuilds carries the runner's name and takes the shards back; no session
  other than the two named lock sessions held a lock in the cluster's
  namespace at any sample.

